<h1>The Configuration Layer</h1>
<p class="tagline">How CLI arguments become <code>Modes.*</code> fields — and how to debug when a flag isn't having the effect you expect.</p>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<h2>
  <label class="section-header">
    <input type="checkbox" class="section-check" data-section="1">
    <span>1. The <code>Modes</code> Global — The Program's Single Source of Truth</span>
    <span class="section-label">(read)</span>
  </label>
</h2>

<p>Almost every piece of global program state in readsb lives in one enormous struct: <code>struct _Modes Modes</code>, declared in <span class="filepath">readsb.h:550</span>. It holds everything from the selected SDR type to gain settings to debug flags to network client lists.</p>

<p>This is a deliberate design choice: one place to look for any program state, no hidden globals, easy to pass to other threads as a pointer. The cost is that the struct is very large — over 1000 fields in a mature codebase like this.</p>

<pre><span class="kw">struct</span> _Modes {
    <span class="ty">sdr_type_t</span>  sdr_type;       <span class="cm">// which hardware driver is active</span>
    <span class="ty">uint32_t</span>    freq;            <span class="cm">// 1090000000 Hz by default</span>
    <span class="kw">int</span>         gain;            <span class="cm">// in tenths of dB, or MODES_AUTO_GAIN</span>
    <span class="ty">int8_t</span>      debug_net;       <span class="cm">// set by --debug n</span>
    <span class="ty">int8_t</span>      debug_cpr;       <span class="cm">// set by --debug P</span>
    <span class="cm">/* ... ~1000 more fields ... */</span>
};

<span class="kw">extern struct</span> _Modes Modes;</pre>

<div class="callout">
  <strong>Initialization:</strong> <code>Modes</code> is zeroed by default (it's a global), then each SDR driver's <code>initConfig()</code> sets driver-specific defaults in its own sub-struct, and the main <code>modesInitConfig()</code> in <code>readsb.c</code> sets program-wide defaults (<code>Modes.freq = MODES_DEFAULT_FREQ</code>, <code>Modes.gain = MODES_MAX_GAIN</code>, etc.). Option parsing then overrides these defaults.
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<h2>
  <label class="section-header">
    <input type="checkbox" class="section-check" data-section="2">
    <span>2. The Three-Layer Option System</span>
    <span class="section-label">(read)</span>
  </label>
</h2>

<p>readsb uses GNU argp for command-line parsing. Three files form the configuration pipeline:</p>

<div class="diagram">  Layer 1 — Declaration (help.h)
    struct argp_option optionsReadsb[] = {
        {"gain", OptGain, "&lt;db&gt;", 0, "Set gain", 1},
        {"debug", OptDebug, "&lt;flags&gt;", 0, "Debug mode", 1},
        …
    };
    → Defines what flags exist, their names, arg types, and help text.

  Layer 2 — Key Enum (readsb.h:1252)
    enum { OptDeviceType = 700, OptDevice, OptGain, OptFreq, …, OptDebug, OptDevel, … };
    → Integer keys starting at 700 so they don't collide with ASCII character codes.
      argp maps "--gain" → OptGain (701) before calling parse_opt().

  Layer 3 — Handler (readsb.c:1678)
    static error_t parse_opt(int key, char *arg, struct argp_state *state) {
        switch (key) {
            case OptGain:  Modes.gainArg = strdup(arg);  break;
            case OptFreq:  Modes.freq = strtoll(arg,…);  break;
            …
        }
    }
    → Translates the key+arg into a Modes.* field assignment.</div>

<h3>How the three layers connect</h3>
<pre><span class="cm">// help.h — the option TABLE entry:</span>
{ <span class="str">"freq"</span>, <span class="num">OptFreq</span>, <span class="str">"&lt;hz&gt;"</span>, <span class="num">0</span>, <span class="str">"Set frequency (default: 1090 MHz)"</span>, <span class="num">1</span> }
<span class="cm">//  ↑ long name  ↑ integer key  ↑ arg label  ↑ flags  ↑ help string  ↑ group</span>

<span class="cm">// readsb.h — the key definition (auto-incremented from 700):</span>
<span class="kw">enum</span> { OptDeviceType = <span class="num">700</span>, OptDevice, OptGain, OptFreq, … };
<span class="cm">// OptFreq = 703 (700 + 3 positions)</span>

<span class="cm">// readsb.c — the handler:</span>
<span class="kw">case</span> OptFreq:
    Modes.freq = (<span class="kw">int</span>) strtoll(arg, NULL, <span class="num">10</span>);
    <span class="kw">break</span>;</pre>

<div class="callout tip">
  <strong>Why start at 700?</strong> GNU argp uses integer keys for both long options (your enum values) and short options (single characters like <code>-g</code> for <code>--gain</code>). Characters are integers too: 'a'=97, 'z'=122. Starting the enum at 700 ensures no collision with any printable ASCII character. Many programs use 128, 256, or 512 as their offset for the same reason.
</div>

