<h2>
  <label class="section-header">
    <input type="checkbox" class="section-check" data-section="5">
    <span>5. Debugging Configuration Problems</span>
    <span class="section-label">(read)</span>
  </label>
</h2>

<h3>The commented-out parse_opt trace</h3>
<p>The very first line of <code>parse_opt()</code> is a commented-out print — <span class="filepath">readsb.c:1679</span>:</p>
<pre><span class="kw">static</span> <span class="ty">error_t</span> <span class="fn">parse_opt</span>(<span class="kw">int</span> key, <span class="kw">char</span> *arg, <span class="kw">struct</span> argp_state *state) {
    <span class="cm">//fprintf(stderr, "parse_opt(%d, %s, argp_state*)\n", key, arg);</span></pre>

<p>Uncomment this to see every option key and value as it's parsed. This immediately reveals: (1) which options are actually being seen by the parser, (2) what value they carry, (3) whether an option you think you're passing is silently being swallowed by a different handler first.</p>

<h3>The device-type ordering bug</h3>
<p>The help text for <code>--device-type</code> says: <em>"this needs to be placed on the command line before any SDR type specific options"</em>. Why? Because <code>sdrHandleOption()</code> is called for every unknown option, and it dispatches to the currently-selected driver's handler. If <code>--gain</code> appears before <code>--device-type hackrf</code>, the gain is processed by the wrong driver (or by the default/none driver).</p>

<p>To debug: add the parse_opt trace above and look for the order of <code>OptDeviceType</code> vs <code>OptGain</code>.</p>

<h3>Gain parsing is deferred</h3>
<p>Notice that <code>parse_opt</code>'s <code>OptGain</code> case only <em>stores the string</em>:</p>
<pre><span class="kw">case</span> OptGain:
    sfree(Modes.gainArg);
    Modes.gainArg = strdup(arg);   <span class="cm">// store the raw string, don't parse yet</span>
    <span class="kw">break</span>;</pre>

<p>The actual gain value is parsed later in <code>modesInitConfig()</code> after the driver is fully selected. The raw string lets the code handle special values like <code>"auto"</code> and <code>"auto-verbose"</code>. If a gain change seems to have no effect, the place to look is around <span class="filepath">readsb.c:1621</span> where the deferred parsing happens.</p>

<h3>Checking what Modes fields contain at runtime</h3>
<p>There's no built-in "print my config" command, but you can add one cheaply. After <code>modesInitConfig()</code> completes, add a temporary block:</p>

<pre><span class="cm">// Temporary: dump key config values</span>
fprintf(stderr, <span class="str">"CONFIG: freq=%d gain=%d sdr_type=%d debug_net=%d\n"</span>,
        Modes.freq, Modes.gain, Modes.sdr_type, Modes.debug_net);</pre>

<p>This pattern — temporary <code>fprintf</code> to stderr immediately after initialization — is the standard approach used throughout the codebase for one-shot config verification.</p>

