<div class="quiz-block">

  <div class="question" data-question="1">
    <p>Q1. Why do the <code>Opt*</code> enum values start at 700 instead of 0?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="There's no 700-option limit. The enum can have as many values as you like.">The argp library supports at most 700 options</li>
      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Correct. argp uses integer keys for both long options (your Opt* enum) and short options (single character like 'g'). 'z' = 122, so anything above 127 is safe. Starting at 700 gives a clear visual distinction and avoids collisions with any printable ASCII character.">To avoid collisions with ASCII character codes used for short options (single-letter flags like '-g')</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="The enum values are not indices into the option array. argp looks up options by their key field, not position.">Because optionsReadsb[] has more than 700 entries</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="This is C, not a version-tagged protocol. The 700 is a pure namespace separation.">For binary protocol compatibility with Beast hardware</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
  </div>

  <div class="question" data-question="2">
    <p>Q2. What does <code>--debug nPS</code> (three characters) actually do?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="Only the last flag would take effect if they overwrote each other, but the handler iterates all characters and sets each independently.">Only the last character 'S' takes effect; earlier ones are overwritten</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="There's no 'nPS' combined flag. The parser iterates the string character by character.">It fails because 'nPS' is not a recognized multi-character flag name</li>
      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Correct. The OptDebug case loops over every character in the string with 'for (int j = 0; arg[j]; j++)'. Each character hits its own case: 'n' sets debug_net, 'P' sets debug_cpr, 'S' sets debug_speed_check.">Sets debug_net ('n'), debug_cpr ('P'), and debug_speed_check ('S') all at once</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="Parsing is done once at startup. There's no interactive mode watching for 'nPS'.">Triggers an interactive prompt that lets you pick three debug modes</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
  </div>

  <div class="question" data-question="3">
    <p>Q3. In <code>parse_opt()</code>, the <code>OptGain</code> case stores the string with <code>strdup(arg)</code> instead of immediately calling <code>atof(arg)</code>. Why?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="atof is available and commonly used for float parsing in C. The delay is not for portability.">atof() is not portable on all platforms readsb targets</li>
      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Correct. --gain can be 'auto', 'auto-verbose', or a float like '32.8'. The deferred parsing in modesInitConfig() handles all these cases after the driver type is known. The driver matters because, e.g., MODES_RTL_AGC is an rtlsdr-specific pseudo-gain level.">The gain string can be 'auto', 'auto-verbose', or a number; deferred parsing handles all cases after the driver is selected</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="strdup allocates heap memory just like atof doesn't. The delay is about correctness, not memory.">strdup() is faster than atof() at parse time</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="The gain is fully parsed before the main loop starts; it's not set on each USB callback.">So gain can be updated on every USB callback without re-parsing the string</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
  </div>

  <div class="question" data-question="4">
    <p>Q4. A user reports that <code>--gain 32.8</code> has no effect when they also pass <code>--device-type hackrf</code>. What's the most likely cause?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="gain parsing works for HackRF. The issue is about parsing order, not an unsupported feature.">HackRF doesn't support manual gain in readsb</li>
      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Correct. sdrHandleOption() dispatches gain to the currently-active driver. If --gain comes before --device-type hackrf on the command line, the gain is sent to the wrong driver (the default/none one). The fix is to always put --device-type first."><code>--device-type hackrf</code> appears after <code>--gain</code> on the command line, so gain is dispatched to the wrong driver</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="32.8 dB is a valid gain for RTL-SDR. The issue is ordering, not value validity.">32.8 is not a valid gain step; only certain discrete dB values are allowed</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="Modes.gain is not zeroed between options. It's initialized to MODES_MAX_GAIN and overwritten by --gain.">Modes.gain is zeroed between option parses</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
  </div>

  <div class="question" data-question="5">
    <p>Q5. Which file would you modify to add a new <code>--my-flag</code> option to readsb?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="argp.h is the argp library header — you don't modify library files. That's where argp_option and argp_state are defined.">argp.h — the argp library header</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="sdr.c handles SDR-specific option dispatch, not general program options. A new general flag would not go here.">sdr.c — the SDR dispatch layer</li>
      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Correct. You need changes in three files: (1) readsb.h — add OptMyFlag to the enum; (2) help.h — add a row to optionsReadsb[]; (3) readsb.c — add a case OptMyFlag: in parse_opt() that sets a Modes.my_flag field.">Three files: readsb.h (add enum), help.h (add option row), readsb.c (add case in parse_opt)</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="readsb.h declares the enum and Modes struct, but you also need help.h for the option table and readsb.c for the handler. Just readsb.h alone is not enough.">Only readsb.h — add the enum constant and the Modes field</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
  </div>

