</div>

<h2>Check Your Understanding</h2>

<div class="callout">
<strong>Scenario A:</strong> You add a new <code>Modes.my_feature</code> bool field and a corresponding <code>--my-feature</code> flag. Users report the flag is silently ignored. What's the debugging procedure?
<br><br>
<a href="#" id="toggleA" style="color:var(--cyan)">show answer</a>
<div id="ansA" hidden style="margin-top:0.75rem; color:var(--green)">
Step 1: Uncomment the <code>fprintf</code> at the top of <code>parse_opt()</code> — run readsb with <code>--my-feature</code> and check if the key number appears. If it doesn't, argp didn't recognise the option name — check the spelling in <code>optionsReadsb[]</code>.<br><br>
Step 2: If the key appears but <code>my_feature</code> stays 0, the <code>case OptMyFeature:</code> in parse_opt's switch is not being reached — check the enum value and the case label match.<br><br>
Step 3: After the argp call returns, add a temporary <code>fprintf(stderr, "my_feature=%d\n", Modes.my_feature)</code> to confirm the field was actually set before the main loop starts.
</div>
</div>

<div class="callout" style="margin-top:1rem;">
<strong>Scenario B:</strong> The help text (<code>--help</code>) shows your new option but running with it gives "unrecognized option". What's wrong?
<br><br>
<a href="#" id="toggleB" style="color:var(--cyan)">show answer</a>
<div id="ansB" hidden style="margin-top:0.75rem; color:var(--green)">
The most common cause: you added the row to <code>optionsReadsb[]</code> but forgot to add the key to the <code>enum</code> in <code>readsb.h</code>. If the key value is 0 (or whatever the compiler gives an undefined symbol), argp might display the entry (from the option table) but fail to dispatch it. Another possibility: the option was added to <code>optionsViewadsb[]</code> (the viewadsb option table) instead of <code>optionsReadsb[]</code>.
</div>
</div>

